#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552849 :question-generator/id #uuid "d30a8225-e019-5eaf-aee1-3d6bc47a224c" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179709.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9471e9d-8fd8-54f6-b2a2-21979387ec44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177418} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaf38e8-dea0-53ca-af4c-7595b6aa19d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177419} {:answer/id #uuid "da81cbb7-1c0d-5fb6-a136-436cce0614d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177420} {:answer/id #uuid "1a417b80-1c7d-54df-a4b9-d83760ab35a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177421} {:answer/id #uuid "548acf66-02a4-5f9b-bf62-28fc428f02f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177422}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7770a3af-79d6-5207-9481-e0e095e0784b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177417}] :question/id #uuid "4163da76-7dc5-52f8-8a6d-5dcabcafe172" :question/math-academy-id "q-122139" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfcc5e2-5fae-5e85-98a0-4b4a8389615e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177484} {:answer/id #uuid "b60f042f-515e-55c8-9669-504d7122f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177485} {:answer/id #uuid "fa827bf5-62aa-53a4-8c0b-0bb5bf842b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177486} {:answer/id #uuid "275b43e3-32aa-59d3-b6f6-d5224224ccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6793c3-401c-517a-8cdb-2cd95d932197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6218cae7-f715-5e6e-b9dc-dfe12bb38c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177483}] :question/id #uuid "8b50d48f-8fc4-5b58-ae9a-175e59cd59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122446" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7542f2f6-b892-5626-afb8-db34cd70e0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254709} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ea6601-5749-54c9-ba36-44e09b3b0dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255681} {:answer/id #uuid "e7df80cd-42a0-564e-887c-f1473180be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255709} {:answer/id #uuid "f5478718-e38f-51df-ba4d-0a7c66983c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffffbcf7-2290-5771-97a7-2e3fca38c6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37df0ad-2178-59ae-82b8-7b50bbb1ab58" :question/math-academy-id "q-122530" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{(\\frac{t}{3})}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba4acff-ed4d-5004-82bf-b89074e988ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254189} {:answer/id #uuid "76b7eee7-6a99-5701-a4bd-ca2f9ad5ab1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254718} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7ab28-aada-5ff7-ae14-4e7c1b105102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255041} {:answer/id #uuid "f299ef39-a3f8-5921-9d4d-66255a05f5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b25ddd-1b34-5ba6-b53a-dc771bfba0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2f457a6-0776-5f23-8cdb-bf0ad17c2cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-122534" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b799c33-4069-5d75-9acf-2c1fb425b3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272775} {:answer/id #uuid "5eec3c9c-1f5e-50d8-ad72-41bdf8012479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273202} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45bf0f-9d2b-5f0b-8d67-58b93e277d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273578} {:answer/id #uuid "9885a09f-ec2b-514c-a625-30bef7ad1a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0269b0-7a87-54a9-bbbd-f720ed0c881a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba25d72c-66e5-5660-8cd6-858347d6e9a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-149714" :question/problem "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c793c767-8ecf-564e-af97-7c0e5166064b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2e31f-1ce7-5e4f-bb9e-8d3e5faf7e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218} {:answer/id #uuid "b13d0e88-37bf-5316-8237-40c1c7c1a505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a101a7-ad66-5ad3-8847-9c8591f60fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4412e87-ff23-50d1-8bf7-efc4e936bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca1dcb5-771f-59eb-81e9-1e595c34e462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398231} {:answer/id #uuid "676387be-6610-5f16-9796-2babd5ce0f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398232} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbb623b-497e-5583-a206-85cecb99534f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398233} {:answer/id #uuid "a43b3ff9-d167-5f2a-a49d-342a3fc83921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d025741-a121-5bf5-a673-350aefe4b1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757cf839-4729-500e-9a5f-1ad15eb1165b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122527" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281698df-9249-5da9-9368-9b2f93cd8848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552791} {:answer/id #uuid "455025c2-db51-556e-a3e6-9fa4b4a79dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t \\left(4 - 6 t^{3}\\right) \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552799} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1ecf92-1397-5d44-97e4-f143aa9d8c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552801} {:answer/id #uuid "4f811054-64ac-515c-b215-4b187de7bc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552805} {:answer/id #uuid "a60d9d4a-69db-5a5f-9245-b3924cba5128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a60d9d4a-69db-5a5f-9245-b3924cba5128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552830} :answer-field/id #uuid "0763297f-2785-5418-b00d-ee5ade6a337c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10654151-77bf-5b54-8ad8-7a9ddd7ea1a1" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(2,0)$ and $(-79,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2275c2b9-bd7c-5d72-90c8-7b26275f195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552788} {:answer/id #uuid "36e310df-bff1-5173-9e88-7181749cd810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 t^{5}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552795} {:answer/id #uuid "77b420d0-67c2-528a-91a7-5fcbbe514595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552812} {:answer/id #uuid "7aee2490-8ebd-5fff-aeba-dd54ccd94877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552814} {:answer/id #uuid "ac98de36-83ed-5aef-b75d-338dc0b2dbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "768 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aee2490-8ebd-5fff-aeba-dd54ccd94877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552814} :answer-field/id #uuid "e31bb3e8-a091-5beb-bbef-ce8a5745dea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4996d341-4274-5583-88ff-0f580b30aea8" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $4x=y$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4x=y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=\\frac{y}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\frac{t}{4})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{1}{4})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{4})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=48 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=48\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)^2t^{3} \\\\ &=48\\left(\\frac{t^2}{16}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=3 t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}(3 t^{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}3 t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23197b07-5a7b-53de-9d9e-c615db2ae1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t^{2} \\left(2 t^{3} - 8\\right) \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552789} {:answer/id #uuid "2df87d88-57a2-5c89-9a85-320711d02d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552793} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0929a7-5013-5e55-aa1e-ae864b350074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552815} {:answer/id #uuid "d080d482-7b0f-5090-b506-abea27a84bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552839} {:answer/id #uuid "ed952ab0-5dec-52bf-9c72-9b41ef3af6ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed952ab0-5dec-52bf-9c72-9b41ef3af6ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552847} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85e1c25-dace-5d43-9b45-c16fb0df76d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ed925b2-2bfc-5cd1-979d-0167eb4f42c0" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{3} - 4$ traversed between the points $(-4,0)$ and $(23,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=t^{3} - 4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t^{3} - 4,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3} - 4)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(3 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce1b2b9-3b87-52e0-a1f6-172a88bf7b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 - 2 t\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552802} {:answer/id #uuid "680810eb-e33e-5d7a-ac2d-3e3d2bcc7f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552809} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a2acf9-a41f-5160-8dc1-756db6ef76d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552834} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c023e8-5a47-5179-b7eb-afe7ce12e7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552842} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bd6f10-f9f4-538e-913c-d22618c6f106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{2} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c023e8-5a47-5179-b7eb-afe7ce12e7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552842} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ecb885d-e56e-5e60-9407-9e9e3fa26bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86be1e77-b581-5d25-9650-3cc553d658e6" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2 y + 2$ traversed between the points $(6,2)$ and $(10,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(2)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}t^{3} \\\\ &=4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0260f071-3897-545d-bb02-440d61d15330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552779} {:answer/id #uuid "17052b32-b23e-5b70-b603-b912587c5543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552785} {:answer/id #uuid "19c6da5f-d3b5-56f5-94b4-f6ad26195c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 t - 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552787} {:answer/id #uuid "87c31bd7-b6ca-5532-b4da-aad42b84c763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 3 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552820} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb372a-4d60-5b14-bc9e-3501041766f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0260f071-3897-545d-bb02-440d61d15330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552779} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee0746c-4281-5a79-87b4-bd2fbd506c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c0214e-a2d8-5339-8aee-c36838439a0d" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}$ traversed between the points $(4,0)$ and $(-8,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=4 - 3 y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=4 - 3 t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 4 - 3 t,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 - 3 t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(-3)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x - 2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(4 - 3 t\\right)-2t \\\\ &=4 - 5 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417a2e2c-c99e-5b7e-89a5-04fd8801fb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552798} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3d15a8-46f5-5bf1-811c-a4f19b6a7124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}t^{2} \\left(4 t^{2} + 6\\right) \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552813} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc7687e-5dff-5e5e-97a9-d31743837c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552816} {:answer/id #uuid "c023fc4c-620c-5620-a1bb-9b3840ec1fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552836} {:answer/id #uuid "ed840fbf-dc53-5f4f-83cf-efc910b3cde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417a2e2c-c99e-5b7e-89a5-04fd8801fb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1224e6c3-08af-592f-b40f-08f63238075d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9eafb873-c81e-5383-b266-5700676268cc" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{2} + 3$ traversed between the points $(5,1)$ and $(21,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{2} + 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2} + 3,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2} + 3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(4 t)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05820e9b-5d3f-5f00-8ab1-5cb728ad058c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552780} {:answer/id #uuid "3912c436-323c-5514-82b7-0a551fe02d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{5} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552796} {:answer/id #uuid "5534bb00-f895-5c7c-97e6-684d212578d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{6}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552806} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a0976d-6042-5f97-9145-cb3e806a614e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\sqrt{10} t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552828} {:answer/id #uuid "c7400a6a-8f3d-57bb-bce2-f39b5a9cf40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7400a6a-8f3d-57bb-bce2-f39b5a9cf40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552837} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae4cf2f-2882-5737-907d-832d0c62119c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa8602a5-ab99-5ec1-a115-7e29b5ef7be8" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{3}{t})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{t})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 9}{t^2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{|t|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=y^{2} e^{x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(e^{3\\ln t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=1\\left(t^{3}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(t^{5})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e8e6cf-6f2d-52c7-9466-9bfe3b894881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552786} {:answer/id #uuid "65b7db08-9576-514c-8058-8a41c58bbf69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552807} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8131b6-7baf-5780-9fa9-322bc7065bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}t \\left(8 t^{3} + 8\\right) \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552821} {:answer/id #uuid "8fba40cf-cdeb-5e12-8408-18012db7f78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552824} {:answer/id #uuid "9370dc17-2d30-55db-b451-380c43d516d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{4 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fba40cf-cdeb-5e12-8408-18012db7f78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552824} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a99906-0888-518b-a404-298ec7ba3467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "add2d24a-5255-5b7c-82c3-e44bf8b960be" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{3} + 2$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(130,4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{3} + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{3} + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{3} + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(6 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(6 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}(4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124cbff1-5e98-5d16-ab1f-cc5dd9bcb262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552784} {:answer/id #uuid "73be819e-c547-5ed8-8ad1-7fbb722f68d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{4}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552811} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8d803f-dbce-540f-ae8b-e4bb8ad0e1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552822} {:answer/id #uuid "b95ec9fe-f64a-57bc-b94e-843559920235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552835} {:answer/id #uuid "e4084919-f343-5591-a6f6-3cbf2ccd120c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124cbff1-5e98-5d16-ab1f-cc5dd9bcb262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552784} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec0c910-12f6-5049-8da0-f391acb979fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6a50f8-dbf0-5c57-9f91-2fc2ad40dfd3" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(4,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{2}{t})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2}{t})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4 y e^{x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 y e^{x} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}e^{2\\ln t} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}\\left(t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=4 t^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(4 t^{3})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8e17dd-b1fd-5b63-9132-ad3a7dd6d991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552797} {:answer/id #uuid "4f69e9c5-3b56-564c-bca3-98cc70036ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t \\left(15 - 15 t^{3}\\right) \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552804} {:answer/id #uuid "66347383-a7b3-50bf-9101-6c7215d0630b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552808} {:answer/id #uuid "8f161683-83c0-50bf-a17a-f24ed9e6c9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552823} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ca4202-33f0-5ef5-aa09-f8e692b0f39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f161683-83c0-50bf-a17a-f24ed9e6c9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552823} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb5ae6c-eb61-510c-8183-96be18ef2c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e43f4bf4-b216-5205-b149-df3643df4708" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(3,0)$ and $(-78,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=5 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5\\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}]}
